---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site-config';
import { SITES, BRAND } from '../../lib/sites';
import { articleJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../../lib/seo';

const canonical = `${SITES.data}/explain/how-to-compare/`;
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Explainers', url: '/explain/' },
  { name: 'How to compare two suppliers', url: '/explain/how-to-compare/' },
];

const jsonLd = [
  articleJsonLd({
    id: canonical,
    headline: 'How to compare two suppliers’ specifications',
    description:
      'A specification comparison is only valid when method, reporting basis and units match. How to compare two charcoal suppliers field by field without being misled by a headline number.',
    datePublished: '2026-10-07',
    dateModified: '2026-10-07',
  }),
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITES.data}/` },
    { name: 'Explainers', url: `${SITES.data}/explain/` },
    { name: 'How to compare two suppliers', url: canonical },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  site={site}
  title="How to compare two suppliers’ specifications"
  description="A specification comparison is only valid when method, reporting basis and units match. How to compare two charcoal suppliers field by field without being misled by a headline number."
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  ogType="article"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-title">
      <p class="eyebrow">Explainer</p>
      <h1>How to compare two suppliers’ specifications</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Two suppliers can both write "ash ≤ 3%" and mean different things. A comparison is
        only valid when the method, the reporting basis and the units all match — this page
        shows how to check that, field by field.
      </p>
    </div>

    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Three conditions for a valid comparison</h2>
      <p>
        Before two numbers can be placed side by side, all three of these must match:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Method.</strong> Ash by ASTM D1762 is not the same determination as ash by ISO 18122, which is not the same as an in-house gravimetric method. Different methods ash at different temperatures and can yield different residues.</li>
        <li><strong>Reporting basis.</strong> A value reported <em>dry</em> and one reported <em>as-received</em> describe different quantities of the same material — see <a href="/explain/test-basis-matters/">why the test basis matters</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Units and conditions.</strong> Percentages are only comparable against the same denominator, and performance values (burn time, ignition time) are only comparable under the same test conditions.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Build the comparison table first</h2>
      <p>
        Rather than comparing prose, extract each supplier's claims into one table with the
        same rows: fixed carbon, ash, moisture, volatile matter, size/tolerance, and any
        performance claim. Add a column for <em>method</em> and one for <em>basis</em>. A row
        where those columns differ is a row you cannot yet compare — mark it rather than
        averaging the two numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href="/compare/">compare view</a> on this sub-site presents recorded values on
        a common field schema, but it can only align fields that the records actually state;
        it cannot correct a missing method or basis.
      </p>

      <h2>Normalise moisture before anything else</h2>
      <p>
        Moisture shifts every other as-received figure, so it is the first thing to
        normalise. A supplier reporting 5% moisture and 80% fixed carbon is not directly
        comparable with one reporting 10% moisture and 78% fixed carbon until both are
        expressed on the same basis. When in doubt, ask both suppliers to restate their
        proximate analysis on a <em>dry</em> basis.
      </p>

      <h2>Watch for the missing fields</h2>
      <p>
        A supplier who omits the method, the basis, the batch reference or the laboratory is
        not giving you a specification — they are giving you a headline. The value of a
        comparison is limited by its weakest documented field. Ask for the raw report, not a
        summary PDF, and confirm the laboratory is independent of the supplier.
      </p>

      <h2>Compare batches, not one sample</h2>
      <p>
        A single best sample proves little. Where possible, request the same parameter across
        three consecutive batches and compare the <em>spread</em> as well as the headline. A
        supplier who holds ash between 2.8% and 3.2% is more predictable than one who
        alternates between 2% and 6% — even if both quote "max 3%".
      </p>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-specs-are-measured/">How specifications are measured</a></li>
        <li><a href="/explain/test-basis-matters/">Why the test basis matters</a></li>
        <li><a href="/explain/how-to-read-a-spec-sheet/">How to read a spec sheet</a></li>
        <li><a href="/compare/">Compare recorded products</a></li>
        <li><a href="/specifications/">All specification methodology pages</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
